  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Typical summertime storm activity will continue \nthrough mid-week. Some storms may produce strong to severe wind \ngusts.\n\nToday, the axis of 596DM H5 ridge will settle over central FL this \nafternoon. Near term guidance indicate that weak vort maxes may \ntrack across the SC Lowcountry this afternoon. At the sfc, recent \nruns of the HRRR indicates that the sea breeze will begin to push \ninland during the early afternoon. By the time the sea breeze forms, \ntemperatures across the forecast area are expected to peak in the \nmid 90s with dewpoints in the mid to upper 70s. SBCAPE may range \nbetween 2000-2500 J/kg, possibly higher in the vicinity of the sea \nbreeze. In addition, forecast soundings indicate that DCAPE values may \nexceed 1000 J/kg. Isolated to scattered thunderstorms are forecast \nto develop between the weak mid-level disturbance and the sea \nbreeze, late this afternoon into early this evening. HREF updraft \nstrength probabilities increase to 10-30% of 30 m/s over Berkeley \nand Dorchester Counties. It is possible a collapsing water-loaded \nupdraft could yield a damaging wind gusts late this afternoon into \nearly this evening.\n\nTuesday: A sfc trough should develop across the central Carolinas \ninto GA. Guidance seems to shows an old MCV sourced from northern GA \nconvection reaching SE GA during the afternoon. However, the sfc \ntrough to the west should draw the sea breeze inland a bit earlier \nthan today. PoPs will be increased across along and south of the \nSavannah River, and adjacent waters for Tuesday afternoon. Given \nmoderate instability and decent DCAPE, it is possible that one or \ntwo of these storms could become severe.\n\nWednesday and Thursday, the main issue will be the heat wave \nacross the region. The hot and humid air mass may result in moderate \ninstability during the afternoon and evening. Forecast soundings \nfeature high LFC with an inverted-V profile, DCAPE values may peak \nin some areas between 1000-1500 J/kg. AI Convective Hazard Forecast \nproducts indicate a 15-30% chance for damaging wind gusts across \ninland SC/GA counties on Wednesday. Deep mixing and ridging\naloft may suppress thunderstorm activity for many areas on\nThursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Hot and humid conditions will return Tuesday and \npersist into the weekend. Heat Advisories will likely be required \nand Extreme Heat Watches/Warnings cannot be ruled out, especially \ntowards the end of the week.\n\nHeat headlines are likely Tuesday and into the weekend as heat index \nvalues soar into the 100-110 range each afternoon through mid-week \nand upwards of 115 late in the week. The daily record highs and \nrecord high minimum temperatures will likely be challenged at our \nthree climate sites (see Climate Section for more details). Given \nthe very mild overnight periods and minimal coverage of \nprecipitation, there will be few instances of relief from the heat \nthis week and into the weekend. \n\nMonday through Wednesday: As ridging aloft builds into the region on \nMonday temperatures will reach into the low 90s across most \nlocations. Combined with dew points in the mid 70s heat index values \nwill likely reach into the 100-105 range this afternoon, warmest \nalong the coast. Heat Advisories have not been issued for Monday, \nhowever a few locations may see a brief moment of heat index values \naround 108 in the afternoon hours. Tuesday and Wednesday will see \nincreasing high temperatures, with highs warming into the mid 90s by \nmid week. The combination of these temperatures with dew points in \nthe mid 70s will yield heat index values in the 105-110 range. Heat \nAdvisories are likely for portions of the forecast area each \nafternoon, especially east of I-95 where the sea breeze will bring \nhigher dew point values. \n\nThursday and Friday: The ridging aloft amplifies towards the end of \nthe week, likely bringing the warmest temperatures of the heat wave. \nHighs on each day will likely reach into the upper 90s, with a brief \nmoment of touching 100 not out of the question. Heat index values \nwest of I-95 will likely reach 105-108 (Heat Advisory) with \nlocations east of I-95 reaching heat index values as high as 115. \nExtreme Heat Watches/Warnings may be required for these\nlocations.",
